    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d88-a3">

                <pb facs="0251" n="117" sameAs="frus:frus1888p1/pg117"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 3 in No. 538.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Rice to Mr.
                            Gould.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Agency Pacific Mail Steamship Company</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">San Francisco</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1887-12-15"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >December</hi> 15, 1887</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Sir</hi>: Referring to our conversation on the
                    subject of differential duties in favor of Del Campo Line to ports in Costa
                    Rica, I have secured three letters from large shippers on our Panama Line on
                    this subject. I find there are no shippers here who do not perfectly understand
                    this to be the case. It is not only claimed by all houses in that country who
                    order goods shipped from here, but is advertised in the Government organs of the
                    country. This surely is a discrimination against an American line which is
                    having its effect.</p>
                <p>Very truly yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Geo. H. Rice</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Freight
                    Manager.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
